B.D8=new A.cC(1,"Take the win","Four of your pieces in a line \u2014 across, up, or diagonally \u2014 ends the game. Before anything clever, check whether you already have three with an open end.",B.f8,B.Eu,3,B.a7,"Three amber pieces sit on the bottom row in columns 1, 2 and 3. Column 4 completes the row. It also happens to stop turquoise doing the same thing from the other side \u2014 but you would play it anyway, because winning ends the game before any threat of theirs matters.")
B.f9=new A.kr(2,"blockNow")
B.Ew=s([0,1,0,2,0],t.t)
B.De=new A.cC(2,"Stop the stack","A column filling up with one colour is the easiest threat to see and the easiest to stop. Three stacked pieces need only one more.",B.f9,B.Ew,0,B.a7,"Amber has three in column 1. Dropping into that column takes the square they need. Every other move on the board loses immediately, however good it looks.")
B.EA=s([0,1,6,2,6,3],t.t)
B.Dj=new A.cC(3,"Stop the row","A horizontal three has two ends. If one end is already blocked, only one square matters \u2014 find it before you look anywhere else.",B.f9,B.EA,4,B.a7,"Turquoise holds columns 2, 3 and 4 on the bottom row. The left end, column 1, is already yours. That leaves column 5 as the only square that finishes their row, so that is the only move.")
B.DG=s([3,0],t.t)
B.D9=new A.cC(4,"The middle is worth more","A piece in the centre column can belong to more winning lines than a piece on the edge \u2014 more rows, more diagonals. Nothing about the rules says so; it falls out of the shape of the board.",B.aO,B.DG,null,B.a7,"Count the four-in-a-row lines that pass through the bottom of the centre column, then through the bottom of an edge column. The centre sits on far more of them. That is the whole reason strong players open in the middle.")
B.DK=s([3,3,3,3],t.t)
B.Dh=new A.cC(5,"Gravity decides everything","You never choose a square, only a column. A piece falls to the lowest empty place, so a square you want may simply not be available yet \u2014 and a square you fear may be out of their reach too.",B.aO,B.DK,null,B.a7,"Four pieces in one column mean the next drop lands two rows higher than the first did. When you spot a threat, always ask the second question: can they actually reach that square this turn?")
B.D_=new A.kr(3,"forkNow")
B.DE=s([2,0,4,0],t.t)
B.Dk=new A.cC(6,"Two ways to win","One threat gets blocked. Two threats at once cannot both be blocked, because the opponent only gets one move.",B.D_,B.DE,3,B.a7,"You already hold columns 3 and 5 on the bottom row. Filling column 4 between them makes a run of three with both ends open, and both ends are reachable right now. They block one; you take the other.")
B.D0=new A.kr(4,"stopFork")
B.Ds=s([0,2,0,4],t.t)
B.D6=new A.cC(7,"Take the square they want","The move that would be strongest for your opponent is often the strongest move for you. Before playing, ask what you would do if it were their turn.",B.D0,B.Ds,3,B.a7,"If turquoise gets column 4, they hold three on the bottom row with both ends open and you cannot cover both. Taking that square first costs you nothing and removes the whole plan.")
B.mt=s([2,2,6,3,6,3,4,4],t.t)
B.Dz=s([1,5],t.t)
B.D5=new A.cC(8,"Do not build their staircase","Your piece fills a square, and the square directly above it becomes available \u2014 to them. A move can be safe in itself and still hand over the game.",B.mi,B.mt,null,B.Dz,"Turquoise has three in a row on the second row, needing either end. Neither end is reachable yet. Play column 2 or column 6 and your own piece becomes the step they climb. Any other column is fine.")
B.Fo=s([2,1,3,2,3,3,6,4,4,4],t.t)
B.Dd=new A.cC(9,"Diagonals are the ones you miss","Rows and columns are easy to scan. Diagonals cut across both and are the lines most games are actually lost to. Check them last, every single turn.",B.f9,B.Fo,4,B.a7,"Turquoise runs diagonally up from the bottom of column 2. Column 5 already holds three pieces, so their fourth square is reachable on their next turn. Dropping into column 5 takes it.")
B.F5=s([0,4,1,4,2,4],t.t)
B.Di=new A.cC(10,"Winning beats blocking","If you can finish, finish. A threat you never have to face is not a threat.",B.f8,B.F5,3,B.a7,"Turquoise has three stacked in column 5 and it is tempting to block. But column 4 completes your bottom row now, and the game ends before they ever play. Check for your own win before you check for theirs.")
B.Fr=s([3,2,0,1,1,2,2,3,3,6],t.t)
B.Db=new A.cC(11,"A diagonal you can finish","Once a column has filled up under a diagonal, the square at the top of it becomes reachable \u2014 often several moves after you set the line up and stopped watching it.",B.f8,B.Fr,3,B.a7,"Your pieces climb from the bottom of column 1 to column 3. Column 4 is now three deep, so the next drop lands exactly on the fourth square of that diagonal. Lines mature; keep looking at old ones.")
B.D7=new A.cC(12,"Threats you cannot reach yet","Three in a row with a gap high above it is still worth building. It costs nothing now and decides the game later, once the column below has filled.",B.aO,B.mt,null,B.a7,"Turquoise holds three on the second row here and neither end is reachable. That is not a wasted formation \u2014 it is a debt the position will collect the moment anyone is forced to play below it.")
B.DJ=s([3,3,2,4],t.t)
B.D4=new A.cC(13,"Count the empty squares","The board holds 42 squares. If you move first you take squares 1, 3, 5 and so on, so on a full board you take the odd ones. Whose turn it is when a key square opens is not luck; it is arithmetic.",B.aO,B.DJ,null,B.a7,"When you find a square that decides the game, count how many empty squares sit below it in that column. That tells you who will be on move when it opens \u2014 and therefore whether the threat is yours or theirs.")
B.EL=s([3,3,3,3,2,4],t.t)
B.Da=new A.cC(14,"Odd rows and even rows","Because the first player takes odd-numbered squares, threats sitting on odd rows tend to favour the first player and threats on even rows the second. It is a tendency, not a law \u2014 but it explains a lot of lost endgames.",B.aO,B.EL,null,B.a7,"You do not need to calculate this every turn. Just notice, when a position gets tight, whether your remaining threats are high or low and whether the columns under them hold an odd or even number of empty squares.")
B.DI=s([3,3,2,3],t.t)
B.Df=new A.cC(15,"Read their last move","Almost every blunder is a move played without looking at the one before it. Their last piece either made a new line or opened a square above itself. Name which, every turn.",B.aO,B.DI,null,B.a7,"Two questions, in order. What line did that piece extend? And what square did it just make reachable? If the answer to either is uncomfortable, deal with it before starting a plan of your own.")
B.F0=s([0,3,1,3,6,3],t.t)
B.Dg=new A.cC(16,"Stopping a stack is cheap","A vertical threat only ever needs one square, and that square is always the very next drop in the column. It is the one threat you can always answer directly.",B.f9,B.F0,3,B.a7,"Three turquoise pieces in column 4. Dropping there takes the fourth square and the threat is simply gone \u2014 no counting, no side effects. Compare that with a horizontal threat, which has two ends.")
B.DH=s([3,2,3,4],t.t)
B.D1=new A.cC(17,"Make them answer you","A move that forces a reply keeps the initiative. If you spend the game responding to them, you only need to be wrong once.",B.aO,B.DH,null,B.a7,"Prefer a move that creates a threat over one that merely improves your shape \u2014 as long as the threat is real and does not open something for them. Initiative is not aggression; it is who is choosing what the position is about.")
B.Dt=s([0,3],t.t)
B.D3=new A.cC(18,"Edges narrow your game","A piece in the outermost column belongs to the fewest lines on the board. Playing there early usually gives away a move.",B.aO,B.Dt,null,B.a7,"There are times to play an edge \u2014 to block, or to change whose turn it is when a square opens. Just make sure you can say which of those you are doing.")
B.Fp=s([6,0,5,1,4,2],t.t)
B.Dc=new A.cC(19,"When you both have threats","Two live threats do not cancel out. The one that can be played sooner wins, and whose move it is decides that.",B.f8,B.Fp,3,B.a7,"Both sides have three on the bottom row here. It looks balanced and it is not: it is your move, so the position is simply won. When you both threaten, stop evaluating and start counting turns.")
B.DC=s([3,3,2,4,4],t.t)
